1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:00,662
Every year,

2
00:00:01,062 --> 00:00:03,838
America now pays about a trillion dollars just

3
00:00:03,850 --> 00:00:04,525
in interest.

4
00:00:04,950 --> 00:00:06,875
That's according to billionaire Mark Cuban,

5
00:00:07,262 --> 00:00:09,487
and he says it's colliding with AI giants

6
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:10,938
borrowing more than they make.

7
00:00:11,362 --> 00:00:13,175
His warning on The Diary of a CEO?

8
00:00:13,588 --> 00:00:15,200
We could be heading into a debt bubble.

9
00:00:15,625 --> 00:00:17,175
But Cuban is no AI doomer.

10
00:00:17,588 --> 00:00:19,025
So why is he sitting on cash?

11
00:00:19,450 --> 00:00:19,888
Because,

12
00:00:20,275 --> 00:00:22,237
he says, the next crash is where the

13
00:00:22,250 --> 00:00:23,412
real money is made.

14
00:00:23,825 --> 00:00:26,925
This is that ninety-minute conversation in ten minutes.

15
00:00:27,412 --> 00:00:27,762
Part one.

16
00:00:28,175 --> 00:00:29,588
Everything is a meme stock.

17
00:00:30,012 --> 00:00:31,825
Steven Bartlett asks him directly:

18
00:00:32,138 --> 00:00:33,225
do you think we're in a bubble?

19
00:00:33,638 --> 00:00:34,512
Cuban says yes.

20
00:00:34,938 --> 00:00:36,338
But not like the dot-com bubble.

21
00:00:36,750 --> 00:00:37,225
This time,

22
00:00:37,662 --> 00:00:39,950
he says, the problem is that everything has

23
00:00:39,962 --> 00:00:41,112
become a meme stock.

24
00:00:41,538 --> 00:00:41,888
Bitcoin.

25
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:42,775
Micron.

26
00:00:43,188 --> 00:00:44,925
Even giants like Google and Apple.

27
00:00:45,338 --> 00:00:47,150
His evidence is how prices move.

28
00:00:47,562 --> 00:00:49,750
A company can miss its earnings forecast by

29
00:00:49,762 --> 00:00:50,588
a single penny,

30
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:52,662
and the stock drops eighteen percent.

31
00:00:53,088 --> 00:00:55,375
The real value of that business didn't change

32
00:00:55,388 --> 00:00:57,025
by eighteen percent overnight.

33
00:00:57,450 --> 00:01:00,062
Buyers and sellers just rushed in and out

34
00:01:00,075 --> 00:01:00,738
on the news.

35
00:01:01,138 --> 00:01:02,112
In Cuban's words,

36
00:01:02,350 --> 00:01:04,375
we're trading stocks like baseball cards.

37
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:07,088
There's no intrinsic value in the price anymore.

38
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:08,650
Only perceived value,

39
00:01:08,925 --> 00:01:10,438
and a guess about what the next person

40
00:01:10,450 --> 00:01:10,875
will pay.

41
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:12,800
That phrase is worth a pause,

42
00:01:13,012 --> 00:01:15,750
because Cuban literally started his career with baseball

43
00:01:15,762 --> 00:01:16,238
cards.

44
00:01:16,650 --> 00:01:18,000
At nine or ten years old,

45
00:01:18,362 --> 00:01:19,688
he bought packs for a quarter,

46
00:01:19,988 --> 00:01:20,825
repackaged them,

47
00:01:21,250 --> 00:01:22,712
and sold them for fifty cents.

48
00:01:23,125 --> 00:01:24,300
So what pops this bubble?

49
00:01:24,738 --> 00:01:25,825
Here's the surprising part.

50
00:01:26,238 --> 00:01:27,612
Cuban says he has no idea.

51
00:01:28,025 --> 00:01:29,900
Even Bartlett admits it's tempting to put a

52
00:01:29,912 --> 00:01:31,362
few dollars into Nvidia.

53
00:01:31,775 --> 00:01:32,500
Cuban gets it,

54
00:01:32,762 --> 00:01:35,762
because this technology really is without precedent.

55
00:01:36,175 --> 00:01:37,262
What he does know is history.

56
00:01:37,675 --> 00:01:39,150
When the internet bubble burst,

57
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:41,425
Dell and Amazon got crushed.

58
00:01:41,850 --> 00:01:44,262
People mocked Amazon for losing so much money.

59
00:01:44,675 --> 00:01:46,162
Today, it's worth trillions.

60
00:01:46,575 --> 00:01:48,475
Anyone who stuck it out did very well.

61
00:01:48,888 --> 00:01:50,075
So there will be winners again,

62
00:01:50,438 --> 00:01:51,012
he says.

63
00:01:51,438 --> 00:01:52,750
He just can't tell you which ones.

64
00:01:53,162 --> 00:01:53,962
Is it Anthropic?

65
00:01:54,375 --> 00:01:55,150
Is it OpenAI?

66
00:01:55,587 --> 00:01:56,150
He doesn't know.

67
00:01:56,562 --> 00:01:58,012
And that uncertainty matters,

68
00:01:58,188 --> 00:02:01,288
because underneath this AI boom sits something much

69
00:02:01,300 --> 00:02:03,075
less exciting than chatbots.

70
00:02:03,488 --> 00:02:03,750
Debt.

71
00:02:04,162 --> 00:02:04,650
A lot of it.

72
00:02:05,146 --> 00:02:05,583
Part two.

73
00:02:06,008 --> 00:02:07,083
The debt behind the boom.

74
00:02:07,496 --> 00:02:09,046
AI keeps getting cheaper to use.

75
00:02:09,471 --> 00:02:11,858
But Cuban says that's partly because the companies

76
00:02:11,871 --> 00:02:14,158
building it keep borrowing and spending to buy

77
00:02:14,171 --> 00:02:15,746
more power and more chips.

78
00:02:16,171 --> 00:02:17,571
The giant cloud companies,

79
00:02:17,721 --> 00:02:19,246
the so-called hyperscalers,

80
00:02:19,608 --> 00:02:19,946
are,

81
00:02:20,321 --> 00:02:21,083
in his words,

82
00:02:21,358 --> 00:02:22,821
borrowing more than they're making.

83
00:02:23,271 --> 00:02:25,196
He thinks Meta is borrowing more than its

84
00:02:25,208 --> 00:02:25,921
cash flow.

85
00:02:26,333 --> 00:02:27,558
And he says Nvidia,

86
00:02:27,946 --> 00:02:29,633
a company he's a huge fan of,

87
00:02:30,033 --> 00:02:32,383
is basically subsidizing half the industry,

88
00:02:32,683 --> 00:02:34,183
because it has the cash to do it.

89
00:02:34,596 --> 00:02:35,508
Now add the government.

90
00:02:35,933 --> 00:02:37,846
The United States keeps running deficits,

91
00:02:38,083 --> 00:02:39,408
so it keeps borrowing too.

92
00:02:39,833 --> 00:02:42,521
Cuban says the country is already paying around

93
00:02:42,533 --> 00:02:44,633
a trillion dollars a year in interest.

94
00:02:45,046 --> 00:02:47,171
And when the government and the AI giants

95
00:02:47,183 --> 00:02:49,421
are all competing for the same lenders,

96
00:02:49,771 --> 00:02:50,896
interest rates go up.

97
00:02:51,321 --> 00:02:52,258
His math is simple.

98
00:02:52,671 --> 00:02:54,921
If you owe forty billion dollars and rates

99
00:02:54,933 --> 00:02:55,671
keep climbing,

100
00:02:56,046 --> 00:02:58,008
at some point you can't raise money anymore.

101
00:02:58,421 --> 00:02:59,458
Something gets crowded out.

102
00:02:59,883 --> 00:03:00,321
Which thing?

103
00:03:00,758 --> 00:03:02,646
He admits he's not enough of a financial

104
00:03:02,658 --> 00:03:03,421
expert to say.

105
00:03:03,846 --> 00:03:05,208
But something, he says,

106
00:03:05,421 --> 00:03:06,108
is going to give.

107
00:03:06,533 --> 00:03:08,071
That's why he once joked that a lot

108
00:03:08,083 --> 00:03:10,158
of today's data centers will end up as

109
00:03:10,171 --> 00:03:11,208
pickleball courts.

110
00:03:11,621 --> 00:03:12,333
It's a metaphor,

111
00:03:12,608 --> 00:03:14,058
not a forecast about sport.

112
00:03:14,483 --> 00:03:16,696
His point is that real progress in AI

113
00:03:16,708 --> 00:03:18,396
should mean less power doing more.

114
00:03:18,808 --> 00:03:21,358
Right now, the industry does more by burning

115
00:03:21,371 --> 00:03:21,746
more.

116
00:03:22,196 --> 00:03:22,933
Here's the twist.

117
00:03:23,346 --> 00:03:25,283
You might expect this from an AI skeptic.

118
00:03:25,696 --> 00:03:27,808
Instead, it comes from the man who built

119
00:03:27,871 --> 00:03:29,096
Broadcast dot com,

120
00:03:29,471 --> 00:03:31,521
back when people told him streaming was a

121
00:03:31,533 --> 00:03:31,958
joke.

122
00:03:32,371 --> 00:03:34,633
So does Cuban think AI is overhyped?

123
00:03:35,046 --> 00:03:35,733
Not exactly.

124
00:03:36,158 --> 00:03:37,383
He thinks it's misunderstood.

125
00:03:37,879 --> 00:03:38,392
Part three.

126
00:03:38,804 --> 00:03:39,817
AI is not smart.

127
00:03:40,229 --> 00:03:41,317
Cuban says it flatly.

128
00:03:41,729 --> 00:03:42,292
In his view,

129
00:03:42,717 --> 00:03:45,317
AI uses statistics to predict the next word.

130
00:03:45,729 --> 00:03:47,592
It's predictive, not deterministic.

131
00:03:48,004 --> 00:03:50,304
Ask four different AIs the same question,

132
00:03:50,617 --> 00:03:52,029
and you get four different answers.

133
00:03:52,442 --> 00:03:53,804
Change your question slightly,

134
00:03:54,117 --> 00:03:56,179
and the same AI changes its answer.

135
00:03:56,604 --> 00:03:56,829
That,

136
00:03:57,229 --> 00:03:59,367
he says, doesn't work in the real world.

137
00:03:59,779 --> 00:04:02,104
AI doesn't know the consequences of its actions.

138
00:04:02,517 --> 00:04:03,879
It has no built-in ethics.

139
00:04:04,292 --> 00:04:05,529
And it's always out of date,

140
00:04:05,742 --> 00:04:07,704
because the moment a model is released,

141
00:04:07,917 --> 00:04:09,567
today's conversations aren't in it.

142
00:04:09,992 --> 00:04:10,979
His favourite example.

143
00:04:11,404 --> 00:04:13,542
Put him at a busy four-way intersection,

144
00:04:13,817 --> 00:04:15,879
with the best AI model on his phone

145
00:04:16,117 --> 00:04:17,642
and a seeing-eye dog beside him.

146
00:04:18,054 --> 00:04:19,567
He'd let the dog walk him across the

147
00:04:19,579 --> 00:04:20,354
street first.

148
00:04:20,767 --> 00:04:22,967
Or picture a two-year-old in a high chair.

149
00:04:23,392 --> 00:04:25,167
The toddler knows that if the sippy cup

150
00:04:25,179 --> 00:04:26,204
goes over the edge,

151
00:04:26,517 --> 00:04:27,317
mom comes running.

152
00:04:27,742 --> 00:04:29,854
Cuban says AI is never going to figure

153
00:04:29,867 --> 00:04:30,304
that out.

154
00:04:30,717 --> 00:04:32,392
Even the scary hacking stories,

155
00:04:32,579 --> 00:04:34,367
he says, aren't intelligence.

156
00:04:34,779 --> 00:04:35,942
Nothing new was invented.

157
00:04:36,354 --> 00:04:38,904
AI just tried every known trick in every

158
00:04:38,917 --> 00:04:40,092
possible combination.

159
00:04:40,517 --> 00:04:41,442
That's brute force.

160
00:04:41,854 --> 00:04:43,967
And defenders can use the same brute force

161
00:04:43,979 --> 00:04:45,504
to find their own holes first.

162
00:04:45,929 --> 00:04:46,742
So he's not a doomer.

163
00:04:47,179 --> 00:04:48,829
He says he's more afraid of what people

164
00:04:48,842 --> 00:04:51,367
do out of fear and misunderstanding than of

165
00:04:51,379 --> 00:04:52,317
what AI will do.

166
00:04:52,742 --> 00:04:53,967
Bartlett pushes back hard.

167
00:04:54,392 --> 00:04:56,392
These technologies look exponential.

168
00:04:56,804 --> 00:04:58,479
His own Tesla drives itself,

169
00:04:58,779 --> 00:05:01,029
and its crash rate is reportedly lower than

170
00:05:01,042 --> 00:05:02,004
a human driver's.

171
00:05:02,417 --> 00:05:03,129
At some point,

172
00:05:03,317 --> 00:05:04,129
doesn't the curve win?

173
00:05:04,554 --> 00:05:05,279
Cuban's answer:

174
00:05:05,779 --> 00:05:07,392
as long as there's a delay between the

175
00:05:07,404 --> 00:05:08,892
real world and the model,

176
00:05:09,167 --> 00:05:10,367
people keep the edge.

177
00:05:10,779 --> 00:05:13,554
And yet, this same skeptic has twenty-five AI

178
00:05:13,567 --> 00:05:14,554
agents on his phone.

179
00:05:15,046 --> 00:05:15,571
Part four.

180
00:05:15,983 --> 00:05:17,433
Twenty-five agents on his phone.

181
00:05:17,858 --> 00:05:19,708
Here's the contradiction that isn't one.

182
00:05:20,121 --> 00:05:22,771
Cuban calls himself the biggest proponent of AI

183
00:05:22,783 --> 00:05:23,321
there is.

184
00:05:23,746 --> 00:05:25,271
He's been in tech for decades,

185
00:05:25,571 --> 00:05:27,896
and he says this dwarfs everything before it.

186
00:05:28,321 --> 00:05:30,896
He runs about twenty-five AI agents that handle

187
00:05:30,908 --> 00:05:32,033
the tedious work for him.

188
00:05:32,458 --> 00:05:33,096
Because of them,

189
00:05:33,471 --> 00:05:35,996
he says, he finally read a non-fiction book

190
00:05:36,008 --> 00:05:38,008
for the first time in twenty-five years.

191
00:05:38,421 --> 00:05:39,246
One real example.

192
00:05:39,658 --> 00:05:40,933
At Cost Plus Drugs,

193
00:05:41,121 --> 00:05:43,471
his company that sells cheaper prescription medicine,

194
00:05:43,821 --> 00:05:45,821
he's fighting what he calls the big healthcare

195
00:05:45,833 --> 00:05:46,608
conglomerates.

196
00:05:47,021 --> 00:05:48,996
Now an agent tells him every time one

197
00:05:49,008 --> 00:05:49,921
of them gets sued,

198
00:05:50,233 --> 00:05:52,046
and every time they bid on a contract.

199
00:05:52,458 --> 00:05:54,871
It even drafts the public records requests to

200
00:05:54,883 --> 00:05:55,621
get copies.

201
00:05:56,046 --> 00:05:56,483
Before,

202
00:05:56,858 --> 00:05:57,796
he'd have had to hire,

203
00:05:58,071 --> 00:05:59,171
train and manage someone.

204
00:05:59,596 --> 00:06:00,483
So it never got done.

205
00:06:00,908 --> 00:06:02,721
Bartlett catches the obvious problem.

206
00:06:03,133 --> 00:06:04,833
Aren't those tasks someone would have been paid

207
00:06:04,846 --> 00:06:05,183
to do?

208
00:06:05,596 --> 00:06:06,371
Cuban admits it.

209
00:06:06,796 --> 00:06:08,321
He might once have hired an intern at

210
00:06:08,333 --> 00:06:10,233
fifteen or twenty dollars an hour.

211
00:06:10,646 --> 00:06:12,746
His argument is that the time saved creates

212
00:06:12,758 --> 00:06:15,733
new ideas, and new businesses that hire different

213
00:06:15,746 --> 00:06:16,121
people.

214
00:06:16,558 --> 00:06:17,971
He calls it creative destruction.

215
00:06:18,396 --> 00:06:20,983
He went to college near Terre Haute, Indiana,

216
00:06:21,296 --> 00:06:23,208
where RCA once made records,

217
00:06:23,458 --> 00:06:25,608
then CDs, then DVDs.

218
00:06:25,821 --> 00:06:26,608
Cassettes are gone.

219
00:06:27,021 --> 00:06:28,133
Money in music isn't.

220
00:06:28,558 --> 00:06:29,946
But he's honest that the path will be

221
00:06:29,958 --> 00:06:31,346
painful for a lot of people.

222
00:06:31,758 --> 00:06:33,833
His advice to his own kids is blunt.

223
00:06:34,233 --> 00:06:34,933
Learn agents.

224
00:06:35,346 --> 00:06:37,571
Anyone who does will be far more productive

225
00:06:37,633 --> 00:06:38,896
than anyone who doesn't.

226
00:06:39,308 --> 00:06:41,096
And the edge isn't the information.

227
00:06:41,508 --> 00:06:43,258
It's knowing which questions to ask,

228
00:06:43,546 --> 00:06:45,083
and judging four different answers.

229
00:06:45,508 --> 00:06:47,333
Which brings us to how Cuban built his

230
00:06:47,346 --> 00:06:47,933
own edge.

231
00:06:48,446 --> 00:06:48,983
Part five.

232
00:06:49,408 --> 00:06:50,646
The garbage-bag playbook.

233
00:06:51,071 --> 00:06:53,108
Cuban's father did car upholstery,

234
00:06:53,408 --> 00:06:55,821
and never made more than forty thousand dollars,

235
00:06:56,046 --> 00:06:57,021
as Bartlett puts it.

236
00:06:57,433 --> 00:07:00,096
At twelve, Mark wanted new basketball shoes.

237
00:07:00,521 --> 00:07:01,208
His dad said:

238
00:07:01,446 --> 00:07:01,996
get a job.

239
00:07:02,396 --> 00:07:04,133
A friend at the poker table offered him

240
00:07:04,146 --> 00:07:06,458
boxes of garbage bags for three dollars,

241
00:07:06,708 --> 00:07:07,758
to sell for six.

242
00:07:08,171 --> 00:07:09,183
Mark went door to door.

243
00:07:09,596 --> 00:07:10,858
If you can sell door to door,

244
00:07:11,258 --> 00:07:12,883
he says, you can do anything.

245
00:07:13,296 --> 00:07:14,546
Later, in Dallas,

246
00:07:14,721 --> 00:07:17,071
he shared a three-bedroom apartment with five other

247
00:07:17,083 --> 00:07:18,721
guys and slept on the floor.

248
00:07:19,146 --> 00:07:20,883
He got fired from a software store for

249
00:07:20,896 --> 00:07:22,546
leaving to pick up a commission check.

250
00:07:22,971 --> 00:07:24,308
So he started his own company,

251
00:07:24,608 --> 00:07:25,583
Micro Solutions.

252
00:07:26,008 --> 00:07:26,533
His edge?

253
00:07:26,958 --> 00:07:28,158
With any new software,

254
00:07:28,533 --> 00:07:30,383
he figured only the person who wrote it

255
00:07:30,396 --> 00:07:31,858
would understand it better than him.

256
00:07:32,271 --> 00:07:34,096
Everyone else would be tied for second,

257
00:07:34,421 --> 00:07:35,333
and he would outwork them.

258
00:07:35,758 --> 00:07:37,283
He read the manuals through the night.

259
00:07:37,721 --> 00:07:39,596
He says it's exactly the same with AI

260
00:07:39,608 --> 00:07:40,008
today.

261
00:07:40,421 --> 00:07:42,833
And that company was profitable every single month

262
00:07:42,971 --> 00:07:43,821
for eight years.

263
00:07:44,246 --> 00:07:45,846
That's his warning to young founders.

264
00:07:46,258 --> 00:07:49,208
Raising money and chasing big sales numbers isn't

265
00:07:49,221 --> 00:07:49,646
the goal.

266
00:07:50,058 --> 00:07:50,971
Staying alive is.

267
00:07:51,383 --> 00:07:53,308
And he's blunt about who should even try.

268
00:07:53,721 --> 00:07:55,296
If you have to ask yourself whether you

269
00:07:55,308 --> 00:07:56,346
can be an entrepreneur,

270
00:07:56,746 --> 00:07:58,521
he says, you probably aren't one.

271
00:07:58,958 --> 00:08:00,496
He sold Micro Solutions,

272
00:08:00,746 --> 00:08:02,596
then built Broadcast dot com,

273
00:08:02,971 --> 00:08:05,171
which Yahoo bought for five point seven billion

274
00:08:05,183 --> 00:08:06,121
dollars in stock.

275
00:08:06,583 --> 00:08:07,921
For anyone stuck in a job,

276
00:08:08,283 --> 00:08:09,421
his advice is simple.

277
00:08:09,833 --> 00:08:11,133
Don't hunt for the perfect job.

278
00:08:11,546 --> 00:08:12,308
Just get a job,

279
00:08:12,596 --> 00:08:13,696
and get really good at it.

280
00:08:14,121 --> 00:08:16,096
Be brutally honest about what you're bad at.

281
00:08:16,508 --> 00:08:18,858
And ignore the get-rich-quick videos in your feed.

282
00:08:19,283 --> 00:08:19,733
His line:

283
